ds the kingdom to Mithridates VI of Pontus</title></g><g class="rf-ev"><line x1="354.2" x2="354.2" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="354.2" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="354.2" y="164" text-anchor="middle">6</text><title>63 BCE: Mithridates VI dies at Panticapaeum; Pharnaces II becomes a Roman client king</title></g><g class="rf-ev"><line x1="450.4" x2="450.4" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="450.4" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="450.4" y="164" text-anchor="middle">7</text><title>45–49 CE: Rome replaces Mithridates, son of Aspurgus, with Cotys I</title></g><g class="rf-ev"><line x1="740.0" x2="740.0" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="740.0" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="740.0" y="164" text-anchor="middle">8</text><title>370s: The Huns overrun the region and the kingdom disappears</title></g><text class="rf-axis" x="20" y="200">438 BCE</text><text class="rf-axis" x="740" y="200" text-anchor="end">370 CE</text></svg><figcaption>Relative power on the Institute's 0–10 scale (each profile scaled to its own peak). Numbers mark the key events below.</figcaption></figure><ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 480 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archaeanactids">Archaeanactids</a> unite the Greek cities around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panticapaeum">Panticapaeum</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">438 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartocus-i">Spartocus I</a> takes power, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartocid-dynasty">Spartocid dynasty</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">389–349 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leucon-i">Leucon I</a> takes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Theodosia</a> and grants <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> privileges in the grain trade</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">310–309 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Civil war between the sons of Paerisades 